            0.104,
            0.286,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "QUADRATIC EQUATIONS"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.874,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "39"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.139,
            0.902,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sridharacharya (C.E. 1025) derived a formula, now known as the quadratic formula, (as quoted by Bhaskara II) for solving a quadratic equation by the method of completing the square. An Arab mathematician Al-Khwarizmi (about C.E. 800) also studied quadratic equations of different types. Abraham bar Hiyya Ha-Nasi, in his book ‘Liber embadorum’ published in Europe in C.E. 1145 gave complete solutions of different quadratic equations."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.261,
            0.902,
            0.318
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this chapter, you will study quadratic equations, and various ways of finding their roots. You will also see some applications of quadratic equations in daily life situations."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.332,
            0.37,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.2 Quadratic Equations"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.359,
            0.901,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A quadratic equation in the variable x is an equation of the form "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "ax^{2}+bx+c=0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " where a, b, c are real numbers, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\neq 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . For example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x^{2} + x - 300 = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a quadratic equation. Similarly, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x^{2}-3x+1=0,4x-3x^{2}+2=0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 - x ^ { 2 } + 3 0 0 = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are also quadratic equations."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.443,
            0.901,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In fact, any equation of the form "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p(x) \\equiv 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial of degree 2, is a quadratic equation. But when we write the terms of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\bar { p } } ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in descending order of their degrees, then we get the standard form of the equation. That is, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c = 0 ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\neq 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "standard form of a quadratic equation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.529,
            0.901,
            0.568
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Quadratic equations arise in several situations in the world around us and in different fields of mathematics. Let us consider a few examples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.581,
            0.709,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 1 :",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Represent the following situations mathematically:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.606,
            0.902,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) John and Jivanti together have 45 marbles. Both of them lost 5 marbles each, and the product of the number of marbles they now have is 124. We would like to find out how many marbles they had to start with."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.67,
            0.902,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) A cottage industry produces a certain number of toys in a day. The cost of production of each toy (in rupees) was found to be 55 minus the number of toys produced in a day. On a particular day, the total cost of production was ` 750. We would like to find out the number of toys produced on that day."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.753,
            0.204,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Solution :"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.778,
            0.549,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Let the number of marbles John had be x."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.804,
            0.7,
            0.822
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Then the number of marbles Jivanti had "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "= 45 - x \\left( \\mathrm{Why}? \\right)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.829,
            0.817,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The number of marbles left with John, when he lost "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 { \\mathrm { ~ m a r b l e s } } = x - 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.854,
            0.891,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The number of marbles left with Jivanti, when she lost "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 { \\mathrm { ~ m a r b l e s } } = 4 5 - x - 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.766,
            0.879,
            0.859,
            0.897
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_16.jpg",
          "content": "= 4 0 - x"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
